this morning on the dish the Houston  eatery Lucille's and its chef Chris  Williams one year ago Williams was  forced to close his southern style  restaurant during its busiest month  after the flooding rains of Hurricane  harvey lucile's has weathered the storm  and is having its best year since  opening back in 2012 the inspiration for  the restaurant goes back three  generations into the family history of  its owner and chef tucked inside the  kitchen of his restaurant chef Chris  Williams prepares the dishes he's  created from scratch having tasted each  and every ingredient but shockingly  Williams prefers his customers be the  first to taste the final product I make  it and send it out look at the guests  reactions I don't know this is my thing  okay  even with his take on the classic pork  and beans oh my gosh that has flavor  pretty good you never know what might  meat on his dish chili biscuits  topped off in cheese and dark chocolate  braised duck confit served atop sautéed  collard greens with a parsnip puree  where did you learn to mix so many  different flavors I was fortunate enough  for my experiences this is all my travel  and I've done it always makes its way to  the plate on a whim Williams spent 2002  at La Cordon Bleu in Austin but says he  never considered making a career in the  kitchen until he backpacked through  Europe cooking was like mine my hustles  my passionate hustle when you say it was  your hustle you mean it was like the way  you'd get earned a quick buck right  didn't matter where I was in Europe if I  didn't speak the language or anything I  could burn so in kitchens it doesn't  matter what language you speak because  we all speak the same when it comes down  after a stint in DC Williams moved home  to Houston to open Lucille's the  namesake of his restaurant his great  grandmother  Lucille Bishop Smith she was a matriarch  to your family right but also she was  just like that that pioneer and  trendsetter and just fearless with what  she was doing a chef a culinary educator  and entrepreneur starting in the 1930s  Smith had her own general store  developed curriculum at Prairie View  college and ran a camp for girls this is  really a shrine to Lucille yeah we call  it a reliquary in his private dining  room upstairs  Williams created a place for all the  relics he found researching his family  this is Lucille's recipe book that's the  treasure chest filled with mid 20th  century favorites candy called desserts  cookies Lucille fed everyone from  heavyweight champ Joe Lewis to Martin  Luther King jr. in this letter President  Lyndon Johnson thanked Lucille for  shipping fruit cakes to local servicemen  fighting in Vietnam but perhaps she was  best known for her own special invention  served in first class cabins on American  Airlines the original instant quick  biscuit so we make the original recipe  the slow three-hour method but in that  industrial time people were looking for  something convenient and quick yeah she  came up with the way to make an instant  and then 30 minutes later you have your  hot rolls does his hot biscuit and his  chili biscuit live up to Lucille's okay  here we go I made them I served them for  seven years  this is getting closer and closer than  this there it's right there now  William's father and mother Connie and  Patricia Lucille's granddaughter used to  work for her at the all-girls camp  Waldemar in Central Texas it must be  something for you to look at him and say  wow he's carrying on the family  tradition here much so obviously  grandmother could have lived to see this  she would have been so proud there's  this discovery now of southern American  cuisine and African American cuisine all  over the globe I mean you love good food  wherever you are you know being an  African American food of Southern  cuisine  this is Americana like that everything  that's you know original about American  cuisine and you know where we came from  it came off the backs of slaves at  southern soul food is the base of the  American food scene now this is now  being recognized for what it truly is  the best food I ever find anywhere ever  goes the food of the poor people I find  that to be the most creative food out  there it's not fancy beautiful fake just  honest and I think I find that beautiful  Lucille's has faithfully served  Houston's museum district since 2012 but  was forced to close its doors during  Hurricane Harvey I came in the middle of  our busiest month and stopped business  for almost a full month his kitchen help  feed Harvey's first responders I know a  lot of people had a  and some really tough experiences and we  want to contribute to do it we paid so  we did never really like made it so part  of the culture of the restaurant which  is what my great-grandmother is all  about William says he remembers visiting  with Lucille during her final years a 7  year old boy helping to feed the  great-grandmother whose days of feeding  others had long past  so if you could have this meal with  anybody past and present who would that  be and why you'd probably be my  great-grandmother yeah not a doubt let  her see how I size up to her and she  would think can you imagine those silly  biscuits are not right I need to let  them rise with another 30 minutes where  you put them in the oven yeah I could I  can see you're just being a  perfectionist
 

